@page "/projects/portfolio"

<ProjectPage Title="This portfolio site"
             Kicker="Live · 2026"
             AccentClass="accent-blue"
             Summary="A serverless portfolio built on the Microsoft stack — Blazor WebAssembly in the browser, Azure Functions behind it, deployed automatically and running at no cost."
             Tech="@(new[] { "C#", ".NET 8", "Blazor WebAssembly", "Azure Functions", "Azure Static Web Apps", "GitHub Actions", "xUnit", "Application Insights" })"
             Links="@(new[] { new ProjectPage.ProjectLink("Source on GitHub", "https://github.com/Ollie1994/portfolio") })">

    <h2>Why build it this way</h2>

    <p>
        A portfolio could be a single HTML file. I built this one as a full serverless
        application because the point was to demonstrate the stack, not to publish three
        paragraphs — and because a site with a real API gives you something concrete to
        talk about.
    </p>

    <p>
        The frontend is <strong>Blazor WebAssembly</strong>: C# compiled to WebAssembly,
        running entirely in the browser. The backend is <strong>Azure Functions</strong> on
        the isolated worker model. Both are served from one Azure Static Web Apps resource,
        which means the API sits at <code>/api</code> on the same origin — no CORS to
        configure, no host to hardcode across environments.
    </p>

    <h2>The constraint that shaped everything: $0</h2>

    <p>
        The whole thing runs on free tiers, and that isn't a footnote — it drove real
        architectural decisions.
    </p>

    <p>
        Static Web Apps' free tier allows <strong>100 GB of bandwidth</strong> and
        <strong>1,000,000 function executions</strong> a month, and critically it has
        <em>no overage billing</em>: exceed the limit and serving pauses rather than
        generating an invoice. Every component was chosen to share that property — degrade
        rather than charge.
    </p>

    <div class="callout">
        <p>
            The clearest example is the contact form. Sending email seemed obvious until
            I worked through the cost model: Azure Communication Services bills per message
            with no free allowance, and SendGrid withdrew its free tier in 2025. An
            anonymous public endpoint that sends one paid email per request is an
            amplifier — anyone with a loop can run up a bill.
        </p>
        <p>
            Submissions go to <strong>Application Insights</strong> as structured logs
            instead. It has a real 5 GB/month free grant and a hard daily ingestion cap,
            so abuse costs availability rather than money. My email address is published on
            the site anyway, so the form is a convenience rather than the only channel.
        </p>
    </div>

    <h2>Treating the API as hostile input</h2>

    <p>
        Blazor WebAssembly runs in the browser, which means everything in the client is
        public — every DLL, every config file, every string constant. And
        <code>/api/*</code> is anonymous and directly reachable, so anyone can call it
        without ever loading the site. That shapes how the endpoint is written:
    </p>

    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>Validation rules live in a shared project</strong> and run on both
            sides. The client's run is for immediate feedback; the API's run is
            authoritative, because the client can be bypassed entirely.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Request bodies are bounded twice</strong> — once on the declared
            <code>Content-Length</code>, then again through a limited stream during
            deserialisation, because the declared length comes from the caller and can't
            be trusted on its own.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Line breaks are rejected in single-line fields.</strong> If those
            values are ever composed into an email, a newline in the name or address lets
            an attacker inject headers. Blocking it at the boundary removes the whole class
            of bug regardless of what delivery turns out to be.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>A honeypot field</strong> catches naive bots, and their submissions are
            reported as successful — telling a bot it was detected only helps it adapt.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Security headers are set at the edge</strong> — a Content Security
            Policy with no <code>unsafe-inline</code>, plus <code>nosniff</code>,
            <code>Referrer-Policy</code>, <code>Permissions-Policy</code> and HSTS.
        </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Keeping the tests useful</h2>

    <p>
        The rule that makes the test project worth having is that
        <strong>function classes are adapters, not logic</strong>. A function reads input,
        delegates to a plain service, and maps the result to a status code. Nothing else.
    </p>

    <p>
        Business logic lives in ordinary classes that can be constructed with
        <code>new</code> in a test — no HTTP types, no Functions host, no Azure. Logic
        written inside a function class is effectively untestable, and the test project
        quietly becomes something that can't reach anything worth asserting on.
    </p>

    <div class="callout">
        <p>
            A lesson from this project that cost me an hour: <code>dotnet test</code> exits
            <strong>0 when it finds no tests at all</strong>. A required status check sat
            green on three pull requests while executing nothing, because those branches
            predated the test suite. Green ticks are not evidence — the run log is.
        </p>
    </div>

    <h2>Deployment, and three ways it broke</h2>

    <p>
        Every push to <code>main</code> runs tests, then builds and deploys. Pull requests
        get their own preview environment. Getting there took three failures, each with a
        misleading error:
    </p>

    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>An exactly-pinned SDK version.</strong> <code>global.json</code> named
            the patch installed on my machine; Azure's build server ships a different one,
            and version roll-forward only goes up. Built perfectly locally, failed every
            time in CI. A version <em>floor</em> fixed it.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>A Windows backslash</strong> in the generated workflow's API path. The
            build runner is Linux, so it silently failed to resolve.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Telemetry the host couldn't satisfy.</strong> The Functions template
            generates Azure Monitor wiring that requires a connection string which didn't
            exist yet. The worker threw on startup and Azure reported only
            <em>"Failed to deploy the Azure Functions"</em>, with no indication of why.
        </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
        A fourth was self-inflicted and more interesting: centralising
        <code>&lt;TargetFramework&gt;</code> into a shared MSBuild file is correct MSBuild
        and broke deployment outright. Azure's builder detects the platform by
        <em>text-scanning</em> project files for that element — it doesn't evaluate
        MSBuild, so an inherited property is invisible to it and the repository looks like
        it contains no .NET at all. That duplication is now deliberate, and commented so
        nobody tidies it away.
    </p>

    <h2>Enforced by the build, not by memory</h2>

    <p>
        Conventions that rely on remembering them decay. These don't:
        warnings are errors and the .NET analyzers run at
        <code>latest-recommended</code>, so a warning fails the build. Every NuGet version
        lives in one file. CI runs the tests and a formatting check, and
        <code>main</code> requires a passing pull request. The two analyzer rules that are
        suppressed each carry a written justification.
    </p>

    <p>
        The whole constraint set — including why .NET 9 was considered and rejected, and
        what happens when .NET 8 reaches end of support in November 2026 — is written down
        in the repository rather than living in my head.
    </p>

</ProjectPage>
